VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-034
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, intractable lower back pain resulting in total immobility and fear of an acute spinal cord injury or severe disc prolapse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate sudden, severe, immobilizing lower back pain. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to rule out Cauda Equina Syndrome, acute osteoporotic vertebral fracture, or severe acute radiculopathy, preventing irreversible neurological deficit prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My lower back has completely seized up, the pain is agonizing, and I haven't been able to get out of this chair for 4 hours.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports inflammatory back pain without X-ray changes historically. Tonight, after a long period of sitting on the sofa, the patient attempted to stand and experienced an acute, severe spasm in the bilateral sacroiliac and lower lumbar regions. The pain is described as a deep, locked ache without electrical radiation. The severe restriction in mobility has caused acute panic regarding a ruptured disc. Denies bowel/bladder incontinence, saddle anesthesia, or leg weakness.
Functional Status: Currently immobile, sitting rigidly in a chair.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, acute pain limiting ability to safely mobilize or reach the bathroom.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 100 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-oriented, visibly in severe pain, holding breath during attempted movements.
Physical Exam: Lumbar spine shows marked paraspinal muscle spasm bilaterally. Exquisite tenderness over the bilateral sacroiliac joints. Neurological examination of the lower limbs reveals entirely normal power (5/5), intact sensation, and normal reflexes bilaterally. Straight leg raise is negative for true radiculopathy (provokes back pain, not shooting leg pain). Perineal sensation is grossly intact (by history and lack of red flags). History reveals morning lower back stiffness improving with movement, normal plain radiographs, HLA-B27 positive.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment, clear path to bathroom once mobilized.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Intermittent NSAID use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Sacroiliitis/Inflammatory Spasm in Non-Radiographic Axial Spondyloarthritis (Mild) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of saddle anesthesia, intact bowel/bladder function, and normal bilateral lower limb power and reflexes.

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation with Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative straight leg raise for radicular pain, absence of dermatomal sensory loss, and bilateral localized SI joint pain rather than unilateral shooting pain.

Vertebral Compression Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of midline spinous process point tenderness, no significant trauma history, and age/bone density profile inconsistent with severe osteoporosis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute immobility)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe, and immobilizing lower back pain, a critical presentation requiring immediate exclusion of neurological compromise (Cauda Equina). Triage correctly mandated urgent physical evaluation. A detailed neurological and musculoskeletal bedside assessment definitively ruled out cord compression, severe radiculopathy, and acute fracture. The presentation was confirmed as a severe mechanical/inflammatory spasm secondary to known axial spondyloarthritis. The rapid administration of potent NSAIDs and physical demonstration of safe mobilization techniques were strictly necessary to break the pain cycle and prevent an unnecessary, extremely difficult ambulance transfer to the emergency department tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAID (e.g., diclofenac 50mg) and guided the patient through slow, pain-free pelvic tilt exercises. Successfully mobilized patient to standing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Set an alarm to stand up and walk around the living room for exactly 2 minutes every single hour. Because your arthritis is inflammatory, complete rest makes it rapidly worse; frequent, gentle movement will literally pump the inflammatory fluid out of the joints and prevent the back from locking up again.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days to discuss continuous NSAID therapy and potential rheumatology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you suddenly lose control of your bowel or bladder, if you develop numbness around your groin or buttocks, or if your legs become profoundly weak and give way.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Inflammatory back pain is classically worse with rest and vastly improves with movement. It rarely causes structural nerve damage, but the muscle spasms can be excruciatingly painful.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the pain begins shooting sharply down past your knee into your foot, accompanied by severe numbness.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is attacking the joints at the base of your spine, causing massive muscle spasms to "splint" the area. The anti-inflammatory medication will turn off this false alarm.
Actionable Steps: Do not sit for longer than 45 minutes at a time. Heat packs will help relax the spasms tonight. See your GP to optimize your medication.

